<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">First Council of Nicaea</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/325">325</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a> · Also known as Council of Nicaea</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The First Council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaea">Nicaea</a> was a gathering of Christian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> called by the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-i">Constantine I</a> at Nicaea in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bithynia">Bithynia</a> in 325. It condemned the teaching of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arius">Arius</a>, adopted the Nicene Creed, which declared the Son to be of one substance with the Father, and set a common rule for the date of Easter. It is counted as the first ecumenical council of the church.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/first-council-of-nicaea.jpg" alt="First Council of Nicaea" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: C messier · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%CE%9C%CE%B9%CF%87%CE%B1%CE%AE%CE%BB_%CE%94%CE%B1%CE%BC%CE%B1%CF%83%CE%BA%CE%B7%CE%BD%CF%8C%CF%82_-_%CE%91%CE%84_%CE%9F%CE%B9%CE%BA%CE%BF%CF%85%CE%BC%CE%B5%CE%BD%CE%B9%CE%BA%CE%AE_%CE%A3%CF%8D%CE%BD%CE%BF%CE%B4%CE%BF%CF%82_7741.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its Jewish communities and, later, a bishop of Bosporus recorded at the <b>Council of Nicaea</b> in 325 linked the region to the wider religious world of the Mediterranean.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bosporan-kingdom">Bosporan Kingdom · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Roman Empire the emperor Constantine, after his victory at the Milvian Bridge in 312, favoured the church; in 313 he and his co-emperor Licinius granted Christians freedom of worship, and in 325 he called the <b>Council of Nicaea</b>.</blockquote><a href="/religion/christianity">Christianity · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>The conversion of Constantine I ended the persecutions; the Edict of Milan (313) made mutual toleration the empire’s ideology, and Constantine funded churches and summoned the <b>First Council of Nicaea</b>.</blockquote><a href="/religion/roman-religion">Roman religion · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>First Council of Nicaea</b>, convened by the emperor Constantine I, adopted the original Nicene Creed, whose text ends with anathemas against Arian propositions.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-19">19 June in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The Second <b>Council of Nicaea</b> opened in the church of Hagia Sophia at Nicaea, the site of the first ecumenical council.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-24">24 September in history</a></li></ul></section>
